  m_Name: COC2_L3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::LessonData
  lessonID: 7
  cocID: COC_02
  lessonTitle: Tools, Equipment and Testing Devices
  description: 
  lessonSceneName: LessonScene
  requiredGoldLessonID: 6
  learningObjectives:
  - "\u2022 Identify different tools to be used for networking"
  - "\u2022 Use appropriate tools to be used in setting up computer networking "
  subtopics: []
  slides:
  - subtopicID: 0701
    slideType: 0
    content: "Tools to be used in networking \r\n\r\nLan Tester- covers the fields
      of installation and network control. Lan testers can determine IP Addresses,
      identify polarity, connected to port and link connectivity. It is used to test
      the strength and connectivity of a particular type of cable or other wired
      assemblies.  \r\n\r\nPunch-down \u2013 also known as krone tool, is a hand
      tool used to connect telecommunications and network wires to a patch panel,
      punch down block, keystone module, or surface mount box. The \u201Cpunch down\u201D
      part of the name came from punching a wire into place using impact action\u201D.
      \r\n\r\nCable Crimpers\u2013 a device used to conjoin two pieces of metal by
      deforming one or both of them in a way that causes them to hold each other.
      The result of the tool\u2019s work is called a crimp.  Modular plugs and coaxial
      connectors are attached to cable ends using crimpers, which are essentially
      very specialized pliers. \r\n\r\nTwisted-Pair Crimpers-  There are two kinds
      of Ethernet cables you can make. Straight through and crossover. STRAIGHT THROUGH
      Ethernet are the standard cable used for almost all purposes, and are often
      called as \u201CPatch cables\u201D  Crimpers for twisted-pair cable must accommodate
      various-sized plugs. The process of crimping involves removing the cable jacket
      to expose the insulated conductors, inserting the conductors in the modular
      plug "
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "A crimper with multiple dies for RJ-11, RJ-45, and MMJ modular connectors.\n\nAn
      Ideal Ratchet Telemaster crimper with crimp cavities for eight- and four-position
      modular plugs.\n\nAn Ideal Crimpmaster Crimp Tool.\n\nCable Ties \u2013 a type
      of fastener, for holding items together, primarily electrical cables or wires.
      \n\nClamp tool \u2013 provides a way to tighten wires wrapped around an object
      and then lock it in place.  \r\n\r\nProtective eyewear \u2013 eyewear that
      usually enclose or protect the eye area in order to prevent particulates, infectious
      fluids, or chemicals from striking the eyes.  "
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "Digital multi-meter \u2013 also known as VOM (volt-ohm- milliammeter)
      is an electronic measuring instrument that combines several measurement functions
      in one unit. A typical multimeter can measure voltage, current, and resistance.
      Analog multimeters use a micro ammeter with a moving pointer to display readings\u201D.
      \r\n\r\nWire stripper \u2013 a small hand held device to strip electrical insulation.
      Wire strippers look more like a notched pincher, with a grab that is adjusted
      with a screw. Strippers for UTP, ScTP, and STP cables are used to remove the
      outer jacket and have to accommodate the wide variation in the geometry of
      UTP cables.\n\nCoaxial Wire Strippers - Coaxial cable strippers are designed
      with two or three depth settings. These settings correspond to the different
      layers of material in the cable. \n\nHeavy-duty coxial wire stripper - are
      specialized tools designed to precisely remove the insulation from coaxial
      cables of various sizes, ranging from manual hand tools to advanced programmable
      stripping machines for industrial use. "
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "Fiber-Optic Cable Strippers -  require very specialized tools. Fortunately,
      the dimensions of fiber coatings, claddings, and buffers are standardized and
      manufactured to precise tolerances \r\n\r\nWire Cutter \u2013 pliers intended
      for the cutting of wire (not intended for grabbing and turning).\n\nPliers
      (assorted) \u2013 hand tools used to hold objects firmly. Possibly developed
      from tongs used to handle hot metal in Bronze Age Europe. \r\n\r\nScrew drivers
      (assorted) \u2013 Used to tighten screws.\n\nPunch-Down Tools - Twisted-pair
      cables are terminated in jacks, cross-connect blocks (66-blocks), or patch
      panels (110-blocks) that use insulation displacement connectors (IDCs). Essentially,
      IDCs are little knife blades with a V-shaped gap or slit between them. A punch-down
      tool is really just a handle with a special \u201Cblade\u201D that fits a particular
      IDC.  "
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "A MilesTek F-type connector installation tool.\r\n\r\nIDEAL DataComm\u2019s
      nonimpact punch-down tool.\r\n\r\nBlades are designed with one end being simply
      for punch-down. When you turn the blade and apply the other end, it punches
      down and cuts off excess conductor in one operation. \r\n\r\nIDEAL Data Comm\u2019s
      impact tool with adjustable impact settings.\n\nThe Palm Guard - is a specialized
      hand splint or protective device designed to prevent finger contractures, protect
      the palm from skin damage, and support rehabilitation in individuals with hand
      disorders or injuries.\n\nFish Tapes - A good fish tape is the best friend
      of the installer who does MACs (moves, adds, changes) or retrofit installations
      on existing buildings.  "
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0702
    slideType: 0
    content: "Cable-Marking Supplies \r\n\r\n\u2022 One of our biggest beefs with
      installed cabling systems (and those yet to be installed) is a profound lack
      of documentation.\n\n\u2022 A 3M dispenser for rolls of wire-marking strips.\n\n\u2022
      Letters, numbers, and icons on self-adhesive strips "
    image: {fileID: 0}
  hasARActivity: 0
